# Intent: wireless firmware update (owner 2026-11-14: the unit is on battery and off USB, "hopefully you can update wirelessly", plus "a special animated icon to show it's being updated"). A new MCP tool self.firmware.update {url, sha256, size, version} pulls one image from the body's one-time URL, streams SHA-256 and size, writes ONLY the inactive ota slot (esp_ota_begin/write/end), checks the image's version string equals the request, sets the boot partition and restarts. NVS and the assets partition are never touched. The new image stays pending-verify (CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE is already on in the flashed bootloader): maipai_update_init() validates it after 15 seconds of idle state with a healthy hub link (no connecting, offline or refused look) or rolls back after 300 seconds; a crash or reset before the mark makes the bootloader return to the old slot by itself. The unit refuses unless idle, battery >= 40 percent or charging, 70 KB internal heap free, and a different version. Events for the host: firmware_update_started, firmware_update_progress (percent, every 10), firmware_update_done, firmware_update_failed (reason), firmware_update_validated. The display shows a turning ring and a thin progress bar at the bottom center (ink colour of the skin, same overlay layer as the song title).
# Upstream: MaiPai only (main/hal/maipai_update.cc and .h, hal_mcp.cpp, hal.cpp, stackchan_display.cc and .h). Applies after 132.
# Check after a flash: tools/list shows self.firmware.update; calling it with a wrong hash answers started and then firmware_update_failed reason hash with the old image still running; a good call shows the ring and bar, reboots into the other slot and the log says the new image was validated after 15 seconds.
